---
// src/pages/faq/index.astro
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  door="faq"
  title="Questions"
  description="Common questions about the skinny union: what it is, whether it is legal, what is new about it, what it asks a member to give up, and who it fits."
>

  <!-- ── Door Hero — Sober Register ────────────────────── -->
  <div class="page-body">
    <section class="door-hero door-hero--faq" aria-label="Questions">
      <p class="door-hero-eyebrow">Questions</p>
      <h1 class="door-hero-headline">
        The questions that <em>come up first.</em>
      </h1>
      <div class="door-hero-rule"></div>
      <p class="door-hero-sub">
        What the skinny union is, what is new about it, what it asks a member to give up,
        and who it fits.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <!-- ── What this is ─────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="page-body">
    <section class="spec-section" aria-label="What this is">
      <div class="col-structural">
        <span class="section-number">Questions 1&ndash;6</span>
        <span class="section-label section-label--cobalt">What This Is</span>
      </div>
      <div class="col-main">

        <h3 class="faq-question" id="what-is-a-skinny-union">What is a skinny union?</h3>

        <p>
          A union whose members focus their collective bargaining power on pay and benefits,
          and leave the rest of the workplace as they find it. It is a bargaining strategy
          that results in a different kind of union.
        </p>

        <p>
          The union represents everyone in the bargaining unit and bargains over everything
          the law requires it to bargain over; on the non-pay subjects &mdash; discipline,
          scheduling, remote work, at-will employment &mdash; what it bargains for is to
          leave the employer&rsquo;s existing practices and handbook in place. So there is no
          grievance machinery to run, no arbitration of discipline, no seniority system, no
          negotiated work rules.
        </p>

        <p>
          Because there is less to administer, the organization is smaller and the dues are
          lower.
        </p>

        <h3 class="faq-question" id="different-from-a-regular-union">How is it different from a regular union?</h3>

        <p>
          Traditional unions treat job insecurity as something <em>to attack</em> &mdash;
          they bargain for just-cause protection, grievance machinery, layoff procedures. A
          skinny union treats that same risk as something <em>to price</em>. It accepts
          at-will employment and directs its leverage at being paid for it. If a job carries
          more risk, the wage should reflect that.
        </p>

        <p>
          A traditional contract covers pay and benefits alongside discipline, grievance
          procedures, arbitration, seniority, shift assignment, and layoff rules. The union
          builds staff to administer all of it, funded by dues. Bargaining power is a finite
          resource, and every item on that list is paid for out of the same pool. A skinny
          union <em>concentrates the pool on compensation</em> and accepts the
          employer&rsquo;s existing arrangements everywhere else.
        </p>

        <p>
          Both kinds of union bargain across the same legally required range of subjects.
          What differs is what they ask for.
        </p>

        <h3 class="faq-question" id="is-this-legal">Is this legal? Does it require a change in the law?</h3>

        <p>
          The model is available under existing law. No legislature has to pass anything, and
          no court has to adopt a rule it has not already adopted.
        </p>

        <p>
          Every tool it uses is ordinary labor practice. The parties&rsquo; freedom to settle
          on whatever terms good-faith bargaining produces, including the outcome that an
          existing arrangement stays exactly as it is. The management-rights, zipper, and
          enumerated-waiver clauses that labor lawyers have been drafting for two generations.
        </p>

        <p>
          What is new is the assembly, and no one has defended this particular assembly in a
          contested case. The drafting is what carries the weight, so any group organizing a
          skinny union should have labor counsel writing the agreement.
        </p>

        <h3 class="faq-question" id="has-anyone-done-this">Has anyone done this?</h3>

        <p>
          The pieces, constantly. As a deliberate bargaining strategy that assembles a
          focused union as envisioned here? Not that we could find.
        </p>

        <p>
          Every tool in the model is ordinary labor practice and some of it is textbook
          &mdash; the waiver clauses, the recognition routes, the rule that a contract does
          not have to change every subject it covers. Labor lawyers use these every year.
        </p>

        <p>
          The combination is the part we went looking for and did not find. We searched
          American labor organizations, the law-review literature, comparative systems
          abroad, and independent commentary. We did not find a union that promotes
          bargaining for pay and benefits alone. Two boundaries on that: the paywalled legal
          databases are beyond our reach, and our claim is about American labor law &mdash;
          abroad, subject-limited bargaining exists (UK statutory recognition covers pay,
          hours, and holidays by default). The nearby cases are addressed in
          <a href="/research">The Full Case</a>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="faq-question" id="is-this-a-union">Is this a union at all?</h3>

        <p>
          Yes, in the ordinary legal sense of the word. Federal law counts as a labor
          organization any organization of employees that exists even in part to deal with an
          employer about wages &mdash; a union built to bargain over pay and benefits is
          squarely inside that definition. And formation has the ordinary hurdles and
          requirements: the employees need to win majority support in an appropriate unit and
          obtain recognition.
        </p>

        <p>
          Its representational scope is not narrowed: the union represents everyone in the
          unit and bargains over everything the law requires, and on the non-pay subjects
          what it bargains for is to leave the existing arrangement in place. That is the
          bargaining strategy that results in the skinny union.
        </p>

        <h3 class="faq-question" id="is-this-a-company-union">Is this a company union?</h3>

        <p>
          No. Company unions &mdash; organizations formed or controlled by the employer
          &mdash; are prohibited by federal law, and that prohibition looks at how an
          organization is formed and run rather than at what it agrees to at the table. A
          union that workers organize, fund, and bring to the employer themselves is theirs.
          The same structure suggested or paid for by the employer is not, and formation
          independence is a design constraint of this model.
        </p>

        <p>
          And the skinny union does not cede ground to the employer. It leaves employer
          discretion on non-pay items in place because that discretion is worth money at the
          bargaining table, and collecting that money is the point. The purpose of the
          strategy is to extract maximum pay.
        </p>

      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <!-- ── What's new about it ──────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="page-body">
    <section class="spec-section" aria-label="What is new about it">
      <div class="col-structural">
        <span class="section-number">Questions 7&ndash;9</span>
        <span class="section-label section-label--cobalt">What&rsquo;s New About It</span>
      </div>
      <div class="col-main">

        <h3 class="faq-question" id="just-encouraging-unionizing">So&hellip; you are just encouraging people to unionize? What&rsquo;s new?</h3>

        <p>
          What is new is the bargaining strategy and the different kind of union experience
          that results.
        </p>

        <p>
          Every union a professional worker could realistically join today bargains the full
          package &mdash; pay and benefits, and also discipline, grievance machinery,
          arbitration, work rules. They arrive together. We went looking for the exception:
          American labor organizations, the law-review literature, comparative systems
          abroad, independent commentary. We did not find a union that promotes bargaining
          for pay and benefits alone. And by missing that strategy, the labor movement is
          missing this structure.
        </p>

        <p>
          The skinny union model is a simpler way into collective bargaining than the one
          currently available. It <em>prices</em> job insecurity rather than attacking it:
          traditional unions spend leverage replacing at-will employment with just-cause
          protection, while a skinny union accepts at-will and spends its leverage on being
          paid for the risk. The aim is higher pay for the employees, through a simpler union
          to organize and maintain.
        </p>

        <h3 class="faq-question" id="why-hasnt-anyone-done-this">Why hasn&rsquo;t anyone done this already?</h3>

        <p>
          Two structural reasons, and one historical one.
        </p>

        <p>
          Most professional workers do not know it is available. Labor law is unfamiliar
          territory for people who have never been in a union and increasingly have no
          relatives who were; membership sits near post-war lows. The legal tools that would
          assemble a skinny union have been available for generations, and the workers who
          would use them have had no occasion to learn that.
        </p>

        <p>
          The institutions that do know how to organize have no incentive to build it. A
          union scoped to pay is a smaller organization &mdash; lower dues, less staff, less
          institutional presence &mdash; and there is no constituency inside the labor
          movement for voluntary downsizing.
        </p>

        <p>
          Nobody with the knowledge has had the incentive, and nobody with the incentive has
          had the knowledge.
        </p>

        <p>
          The historical reason: the full package was assembled in the 1940s, when wartime
          wage controls pushed bargaining away from cash and toward everything else, and it
          has been the template since. It was never chosen from a menu.
        </p>

        <h3 class="faq-question" id="why-not-an-existing-union">Why wouldn&rsquo;t an existing union just offer this?</h3>

        <p>
          Union staff is funded by dues, and dues scale with membership and with the breadth
          of services provided. The grievance function &mdash; processing member complaints,
          representing workers in disciplinary proceedings, pursuing arbitration &mdash; is
          administratively intensive and employs significant staff. A union that concentrated
          its bargaining on wages and benefits would be leaner and cheaper to run, and also
          smaller in staff, revenue, and institutional presence.
        </p>

        <p>
          These are incentives, and they operate whether or not anyone intends them.
        </p>

        <p>
          An existing union that decided to offer the model would solve several of its
          hardest problems at once &mdash; staff, treasury, legal capacity, experience
          running elections. We would regard that as a good outcome, and the project is
          designed to remain adoptable.
        </p>

      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <!-- ── What you give up ─────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="page-body">
    <section class="spec-section" aria-label="What you give up">
      <div class="col-structural">
        <span class="section-number">Questions 10&ndash;11</span>
        <span class="section-label section-label--cobalt">What You Give Up</span>
      </div>
      <div class="col-main">

        <h3 class="faq-question" id="what-dont-i-get">What don&rsquo;t I get?</h3>

        <p>
          Everything that is not pay and benefits.
        </p>

        <p>
          No grievance procedure. No arbitration of discipline. No just-cause protection. No
          seniority rules, no negotiated work rules, no bargained control over scheduling,
          remote-work policy, or restructuring. Your employee handbook governs those
          subjects, and your employer keeps the discretion over them that it has now.
        </p>

        <p>
          One more, and for many professionals it is the biggest: once a union represents
          your unit, you no longer negotiate your own terms one-on-one with your employer
          &mdash; that is how exclusive representation works, and it covers all conditions of
          employment, not only pay. A contract can expressly preserve individual negotiation
          above the negotiated floors, but that term has to be bargained for.
        </p>

        <p>
          For the term of the contract, all of that is the deal. At renewal, scope is
          bargainable, as it is in any collective bargaining relationship, and both sides
          know it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="faq-question" id="give-up-job-security">Why would a union give up bargaining for job security?</h3>

        <p>
          For better pay.
        </p>

        <p>
          The skinny union makes the most sense for workers who are not aggrieved over how
          their employer handles non-pay matters, but who do want more pay &mdash; and who
          are tired of leaving their collective leverage on the sidelines when it could be
          used to fix the pay problem. Every ounce of leverage a union spends winning
          just-cause protection and grievance machinery is leverage not spent on wages. For
          workers whose overwhelming issue is pay, spending it anywhere else is the
          giving-up.
        </p>

      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Fit ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="page-body">
    <section class="spec-section" aria-label="Fit">
      <div class="col-structural">
        <span class="section-number">Question 12</span>
        <span class="section-label section-label--cobalt">Fit</span>
      </div>
      <div class="col-main">

        <h3 class="faq-question" id="who-is-this-for">Who is this for? Would it work at my workplace?</h3>

        <p>
          The model is designed to appeal to professionals whose overwhelming issue at work
          is pay. These are most likely professionals working in liquid job markets, in large
          urban centers with statutory protections well above the federal baseline, who are
          not primarily worried about the way their employer handles job security and other
          non-pay items, but who are paid below what a union could achieve for them. IT and
          networking professionals, program officers and administrators at large nonprofits,
          lab technicians, software engineers, private school teachers.
        </p>

        <p>
          It is a poor fit where physical safety, shift assignment, overtime allocation,
          minimum staffing, or arbitrary discipline are live concerns. The traditional model
          exists for those workplaces and handles them better. That is why we think
          private-sector unionization has concentrated where it has &mdash; utilities,
          manufacturing, construction, warehousing, trucking &mdash; and why it ought to
          expand there.
        </p>

        <p>
          Whether it would work at your particular workplace is not something we can tell
          you. No one has run a skinny union yet. What we can describe is the conditions the
          model was designed for, and that is the list above. The reasoning behind each of
          them is set out in <a href="/research">The Full Case</a>.
        </p>

      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

</BaseLayout>
